"A Plausible Explanation Is Enough to Mislead Multimodal Judges (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22631v1)\nciteturn41306view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22631v1\",\"lineno\":480}); Total lines: 1082\nL368: ### A.2 Benchmarked models\nL369: \nL370: We have benchmarked 14 models: the Gemini 3 family (3 Flash, 3.1 Flash-Lite, 3.1 Pro) ^{1}^{1} 1 cite201†https://deepmind.google/models/model-cards†deepmind.google , the Gemma3-it family (4b/12b/27b) (cite202†Kamath et al., 2025 ), the GPT-5.4 family (base, mini) (cite203†Singh et al., 2025 ), GPT-5.6 Luna, the InternVL3.5 family (8b/14b) (cite204†Wang et al., 2025 ), and the Qwen3-VL family (2b/8b/32b) (cite205†Yang et al., 2025 ).\nL371: \nL372: The download links are as follows:\nL373: \nL374:   * •\nL375: gemma3-4b-it: cite206†https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-3-4b-it†huggingface.co L376: \nL377:   * •\nL378: \nL379: gemma3-12b-it: cite207†https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-3-12b-it†huggingface.co L380: \nL381:   * •\nL382: \nL383: gemma3-27b-it: cite208†https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-3-27b-it†huggingface.co L384: \nL385:   * •\nL386: \nL387: GPT-5.4: cite209†https://platform.openai.com/docs/models†platform.openai.com L388: \nL389:   * •\nL390: \nL391: GPT-5.4-mini: cite209†https://platform.openai.com/docs/models†platform.openai.com L392: \nL393:   * •\nL394: GPT-5.6-Luna: cite209†https://platform.openai.com/docs/models†platform.openai.com L395: \nL396:   * •\nL397: \nL398: Gemini-3.1-Pro: cite210†https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/gemini/3-1-pro†docs.cloud.google.com L399: \nL400:   * •\nL401: \nL402: Gemini-3-Flash: cite211†https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/gemini/3-flash†docs.cloud.google.com L403: \nL404:   * •\nL405: \nL406: Gemini-3.1-Flash-Lite: cite212†https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/gemini/3-1-flash-lite†docs.cloud.google.com L407:   * •\nL408: \nL409: InternVL3.5-8b: cite213†https://huggingface.co/OpenGVLab/InternVL3_5-8B†huggingface.co L410: \nL411:   * •\nL412: \nL413: InternVL3.5-14b: cite214†https://huggingface.co/OpenGVLab/InternVL3_5-14B†huggingface.co L414: \nL415:   * •\nL416: \nL417: Qwen3-VL-2B-Instruct: cite215†https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-VL-2B-Instruct†huggingface.co L418: \nL419:   * •\nL420: \nL421: Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct: cite216†https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct†huggingface.co L422: \nL423:   * •\nL424: \nL425: Qwen3-VL-32B-Instruct: cite217†https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-VL-32B-Instruct†huggingface.co L426: ### A.3 Source Datasets\nL427: \nL428: The datasets used in this work are released under the following licenses: VQA-RAD under CC0-1.0, PMC-VQA under CC BY-SA, ChartQA under GPL-3.0, ChartQAPro under MIT, HaloQuest under Apache-2.0, and PRE-HAL under CC-BY-4.0.\nL429: \nL430:   * •\nL431: \nL432: VQA-RAD: cite218†https://huggingface.co/datasets/flaviagiammarino/vqa-rad†huggingface.co L433: \nL434:   * •\nL435: \nL436: PMC-VQA: cite219†https://huggingface.co/datasets/RadGenome/PMC-VQA†huggingface.co L437: \nL438:   * •\nL439: ChartQA: cite220†https://huggingface.co/datasets/HuggingFaceM4/ChartQA†huggingface.co L440: \nL441:   * •\nL442: \nL443: ChartQAPro: cite221†https://huggingface.co/datasets/ahmed-masry/ChartQAPro†huggingface.co L444: \nL445:   * •\nL446: \nL447: HaloQuest: cite222†https://huggingface.co/datasets/johko/HaloQuest†huggingface.co L448: \nL449:   * •\nL450: \nL451: PRE-HAL: cite223†https://huggingface.co/datasets/TerryHWong/PRE-HAL†huggingface.co L452: ### A.4 Instructions for MLLM Judges\nL453: \nL454: ### A.5 Instructions for Explanation Generators\nL455: \nL456: ## Appendix B Dataset Details\nL457: \nL458: ### B.1 Query Selection\nL459: #### Models do VQA for Query Collection.\nL460: \nL461: VQA was performed using temperature = 0.2 and do_sample = True. The following models perform VQA only for query profiling and filtering. The candidate answers and explanations that judges evaluate are generated separately (§cite11†2.3 ); Appendix cite67†B.6 gives examples.\nL462: \nL463:   * •\nL464: \nL465: GPT-5.4: cite209†https://platform.openai.com/docs/models†platform.openai.com L466: \nL467:   * •\nL468: \nL469: gemma3-27b-it: cite208†https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-3-27b-it†huggingface.co L470: \nL471:   * •\nL472: Qwen2.5-VL-72b: cite224†https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen2.5-VL-72B-Instruct†huggingface.co L473: \nL474:   * •\nL475: \nL476: Qwen3-VL-32B-Instruct: cite217†https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-VL-32B-Instruct†huggingface.co L477: #### Screening model details.\nL478: \nL479: We use GPT-5.4 with extended thinking (effort = xhigh) as screening model to flag low-quality images, ambiguous questions, and erroneous ground-truth labels.\nL480: \nL481:   * •\nL482: \nL483: GPT-5.4: cite209†https://platform.openai.com/docs/models†platform.openai.com L484: #### Answer Correctness Labeling.\nL485: For each candidate answer $A$, we assign the correctness label $y\\in\\{\\text{correct},\\text{incorrect}\\}$ by comparing $A$ against the source-dataset ground-truth answer $g$. The comparison runs a per-dataset cascade that applies deterministic rules in a fixed order and defers to an LLM grader only when no rule resolves the case. The grader is Qwen3-VL-32B-Instruct run text-only: it receives $A$ and $g$ and decides whether they carry the same meaning, ignoring the image. The deterministic rules are:\nL486: \nL487:   * •\nL488: Numeric: $A$ and $g$ match within $5\\%$ relative tolerance (zero requires exact equality), with percent and decimal forms treated as equivalent.\nL489: \nL490:   * •\nL491: \nL492: Yes/no: both reduce to a yes/no value and must agree.\nL493: \nL494:   * •\nL495: \nL496: Exact: $A$ and $g$ match after lowercasing and removing punctuation.\nL497: \nL498:   * •\nL499: \nL500: Option: multiple-choice answers match by option text or by option letter.\nL501: Each dataset applies a subset in order. HaloQuest carries several ground-truth answers per query, where the grader accepts $A$ when it matches any one of them. We use no curated synonym table; domain equivalences (e.g., “cardiomegaly” vs. “enlarged heart”) rest on the grader’s semantic judgment.\nL502: #### Induced-wrong set construction.\nL503: \nL504: We first select the queries that all but at most one profiling model answer correctly, then use GPT-5.4 mini to deliberately produce wrong answers together with reasoning traces that support them. We paraphrase these traces into persuasive explanations with the same 41 persuasion techniques and the same paraphrasing procedure as the main set.\nL505: \nL506: ### B.2 Explanation Conditions\nL507: #### Persuasion Techniques\nL508: \nL509: Table cite119†4 lists the 41 techniques with their strategy category and ethical label, alongside their per-technique persuasion effects. Full per-technique definitions are in the released codebase.\nL510:  |  |  |  | $\\Delta$CRR $\\uparrow$  | $\\Delta$IRR\nL511: ID  | Technique  | Strategy  | Eth.  | VI  | TO  | VI  | TO\nL512: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL513: 32  | Threats  | Threat  | $\\times$  | 15.2  | 14.0  | 11.3  | 11.1\nL514: 1  | Evidence-based Persuasion  | Information-based  | ✓  | 14.9  | 14.0  | 12.4  | 11.7\nL515: 41  | Information-dense  | Information-based  | ✓  | 14.4  | 14.0  | 12.4  | 11.9\nL516: 40  | Discouragement  | Social-sabotage  | $\\times$  | 14.4  | 15.2  | 12.4  | 10.8\nL517: 12  | Complimenting  | Relationship-based  | ✓  | 14.3  | 12.8  | 11.4  | 11.1\nL518: 16  | Favor  | Exchange-based  | ✓  | 14.3  | 13.3  | 11.2  | 11.0\nL519: 9  | Door-in-the-face Commitment  | Commitment-based  | ✓  | 14.3  | 13.0  | 12.2  | 13.4\nL520: 27  | Reciprocity  | Exchange-based  | ✓  | 14.2  | 12.3  | 11.2  | 11.0\nL521: 39  | Exploiting Weakness  | Social