ion  | Exchange-based  | ✓  | 11.9  | 11.7  | 11.5  | 10.6\nL545: 5  | Authority Endorsement  | Credibility-based  | ✓  | 11.8  | 11.2  | 10.9  | 10.0\nL546: 25  | Framing  | Information-bias  | ✓  | 11.7  | 12.2  | 10.5  | 9.8\nL547: 22  | Storytelling  | Emotion-based  | ✓  | 11.6  | 12.1  | 9.2  | 9.5\nL548: 36  | Rumors  | Social-sabotage  | $\\times$  | 11.2  | 10.8  | 10.0  | 8.3\nL549: 6  | Social Proof  | Norm-based  | ✓  | 11.2  | 10.1  | 10.1  | 10.6\nL550: 23  | Anchoring  | Information-bias  | ✓  | 11.1  | 11.9  | 11.1  | 9.7\nL551: 4  | Non-expert Testimonial  | Credibility-based  | ✓  | 10.9  | 9.3  | 10.2  | 9.4\nL552: 29  | Supply Scarcity  | Scarcity-based  | ✓  | 10.2  | 10.1  | 9.8  | 9.0\nL553: 20  | Positive Emotional Appeal  | Emotion-based  | ✓  | 9.1  | 8.5  | 8.2  | 9.0\nL554:  | Mean  |  |  | 12.7  | 12.3  | 10.9  | 10.6\nL555: Table 4: The 41 persuasion techniques instantiated in the Persuasion conditions (§cite11†2.3 ) and their per-technique persuasion effect. Strategy and ethical labels follow the source taxonomy of cite99†Zeng et al. (2024) ; technique 41 (Information-dense) is adapted from cite100†Hackenburg et al. (2025) ; ✓and $\\times$ mark techniques the taxonomy labels ethical and unethical.\nL556: Effect columns give the mean drop in correct rejection rate ($\\Delta$CRR, $\\uparrow$ larger is stronger degradation) and incorrect rejection rate ($\\Delta$IRR), in percentage points relative to the no-explanation baseline, averaged over the 14 judges, under vision-informed (VI) and text-only (TO) persuasion. Techniques are sorted by $\\Delta$CRR under VI.\nL557: ### B.3 Explanation Text-Quality Metrics\nL558: Condition  | $n$  | Perplexity $\\downarrow$  | Readability  | Distinct-1  | Distinct-2  | NLI Ent. $\\uparrow$\nL559: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL560: Vacuous  | ctrl  | 32.25  | 16.66  | .818  | .987  | .002\nL561: Mismatch  | ctrl  | 52.75  | 10.55  | .731  | .963  | .032\nL562: Plain  | 180  | 45.03  | 9.86  | .712  | .949  | .695\nL563: CoT  | 180  | 53.33  | 10.12  | .726  | .960  | .489\nL564: Persuasion (VI)  | 7,380  | 60.15  | 11.11  | .740  | .969  | .595\nL565: Persuasion (TO)  | 7,380  | 59.05  | 11.16  | .735  | .967  | .597\nL566: Table 5: Text-quality metrics across explanation conditions on the main set. Perplexity and NLI Entailment arrows indicate the preferred direction. Vacuous and Mismatch serve as control conditions with fixed or shuffled text, hence $n$ is marked “ctrl.”\nL567: We measure four text-quality metrics across the explanation conditions to test whether the reliability shifts of §cite19†3.1 track text quality rather than explanation content.\nL568: #### Metrics.\nL569: \nL570:   * •\nL571: \nL572: Perplexity (lower is more fluent): mean per-token cross-entropy under GPT-2 (cite225†Radford et al., 2019 ), exponentiated, with texts truncated to 1,024 tokens.\nL573: \nL574:   * •\nL575: \nL576: Readability: Flesch-Kincaid grade level (cite226†Fishburne Jr et al., 1975 ), the U.S. school grade a reader needs, estimated from sentence length and syllable count.\nL577: \nL578:   * •\nL579: \nL580: Lexical diversity (Distinct-1/2, higher is more varied): the fraction of unique unigrams and bigrams, pooled per condition (cite227†Li et al., 2016 ).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBefore Retrieval Begins: Safety and Tool-Use Decisions in LLM Agents Warning: This paper contains model-generated content related to harmful behaviors, included for research purposes. (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23071v1)\nciteturn41306view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23071v1\",\"lineno\":146}); Total lines: 360\nL128: To test the effect of removing a direction, we project a unit direction $\\hat{d}$ out of the residual stream at every layer, position, and generation step:\nL129: \nL130:  | $$\\Pi^{\\perp}_{\\hat{d}}\\,x\\;=\\;x-(\\hat{d}^{\\top}x)\\,\\hat{d}.$$  |  | (4)\nL131: \nL133: We estimate the text-refusal direction $d_{\\text{text}}$ with the tool removed. It is the equal-weight average of within-condition mean differences between safety refusals and compliant responses; capability-related refusals are excluded. For the text-refusal comparison, we steer $d_{\\text{text}}$ and $d_{c}$ at matched calibrated doses and subtract the matched random-direction effect (Appendix cite26†A.4 ).\nL134: The primary generalization test uses five outer behavior folds. Within each outer-training split, four-fold behavior-grouped cross-validation selects the $d_{B}$ layer. We then freeze $d_{B}$ and its orthogonal random control before intervening on the outer-test behaviors. The held-out estimand is the admission-rate difference between target-direction and random-direction removal, paired by behavior, with behavior-cluster bootstrap intervals (Appendix cite26†A.4 ).\nL135: Appendix cite27†A.5 specifies the resampling unit, aggregation order, and selection handling.\nL136: ## 4 Results\nL137: ### 4.1 Why Must Admission Be Evaluated on Both Branches?\nL138: \nL139: Calling the retrieval tool is only one branch of an agent’s response. A model can also withhold the call and answer from parametric knowledge. We therefore measure harm after both decisions with the same judge. Table cite78†1 shows how harm divides across the call and no-call pathways.\nL140: Tool Admission Rate (%) Response Harm Rate (%) Model Harmful Benign After Call Via Call Without Call Admit Harm Granite-3.1-8B 44.3 100.0 68.7 30.4 12.2 42.6 Hermes-3-Llama-3.1-8B 84.5 99.7 76.8 64.9 4.7 69.6 Llama-3.1-8B 70.3 100.0 60.1 42.2 0.0 42.2 Llama-3.2-3B 64.2 100.0 51.1 32.8 0.0 32.8 Mistral-7B 7.8 95.3 100.0 7.8 59.5 67.2 Mistral-Nemo-12B 89.9 100.0 86.8 78.0 0.7 78.7 Qwen2.5-7B 69.6 99.3 78.2 54.4 1.4 55.7\nL141: Table 1: Admission and response harm (%), evaluated on 295–296 behaviors. Harmful and benign admission are tool-calling rates. Admit harm sums the call and no-call pathways.\nL142: Withholding the call does not always prevent harm. Five models sometimes answer from parametric knowledge after making no call. Mistral-7B does so on 64.5% of its 273 no-call responses. Adding this branch raises its Admit harm from 7.8% to 67.2%. Across all seven models, the no-call branch adds 11.2 percentage points to the mean Admit harm. Evaluating only tool-mediated harm can therefore substantially understate risk. The requested task changes admission even when the source is held fixed.\nL143: Pairing the same URL with a benign summarization request raises tool calling to 95–100%. Three closed models show the same contrast: they admit 21–44% of harmful requests and 93–99% of matched benign requests (Table cite79†8 ). This control shows that the low admission rates on harmful requests are not caused by a general inability to use the tool.\nL144: The externally supplied URL could make tool use unusually easy. We therefore remove it and ask the model only for the harmful behavior while leaving the retrieval tool available. Six of seven models autonomously generate a URL and call the tool on 16.2–58.1% of requests; Mistral-7B does so on 0.7% (Table cite80†10 ). Harmful retrieval admission therefore also occurs when the model must initiate retrieval. The fixed-URL protocol remains the controlled primary setting.\nL145: We next test whether simple deployment defenses reduce harmful admission. A safety warning, a restricted tool description, or both reduce harmful admission by 14.2 percentage points on average across the six models with complete runs. The combined defense still leaves 6–72% of harmful requests admitted (Table cite80†10 ). Safety framing helps, but does not make admission reliable. We therefore isolate which part of the request most affects the decision.\nL146: ### 4.2 Which Part of a Harmful Request Most Affects Admission?\nL147: The defense results show that safety framing changes admission but does not eliminate it. We next ask which part of the user request has the largest effect. We separate each request into a harmful domain phrase, a requested-operation phrase, and a specificity modifier. A $2\\times 2\\times 2$ factorial varies these components across 147 behaviors while keeping the URL fixed. The original request forms one condition; the other seven neutralize or remove selected components (Appendix cite24†A.2 ).\nL148: Within this construction, the requested-operation component has the largest macro-averaged main effect (Figure cite81†2 ). Neutralizing it changes admission by 19 percentage points, compared with 8 points for domain and 7 for specificity. The operation effects are heterogeneous across models: $-6.1$ points for Mistral-7B, 2.6 for Mistral-Nemo-12B, 10.9 for Hermes-3, 19.2 for Llama-3.1-8B, 31.6 for Qwen2.5-7B, 35.4 for Granite-3.1-8B, and 40.1 for Llama-3.2-3B (Table cite82†16 ).\nL149: The component ordering holds for six open-weight models; Mistral-7B reverses the operation effect from a low neutral baseline. It also holds in GPT-4.1, Gemini-2.5-Flash, and Claude-Haiku-4.5, where the operation effect is 22.8–51.0 points. On the 86 behaviors for which an independent role audit accepts all three components, the effects remain 19.1 points for operation, 7.8 for domain, and 7.1 for specificity (operation 95% CI [15.3, 23.0]; Table cite83†14 ).\nL150: A topic-matched benign control keeps the harmful-topic URL and prompt skeleton fixed, then changes the user goal to a protective, educational, fact-checking, or counter-messaging task. Six models admit 99.7–100.0% of these requests. Mistral-7B admits 33.4%, consistent with its broader low-admission behavior (Table cite84†12 ). This control weakens a topic-only explanation and supports sensitivity to the requested operation.\nL151: Together, the factorial and topic-matched control show that the requested-operation component produces the largest admission contrast in this construction and that topic alone is insufficient. This shifts safeguard design from topic-only screening toward evaluating the user’s requested goal.\nL152: The generated manipulations jointly change meaning, actionability, lexical content, and sometimes naturalness; we therefore interpret the 19-point result as a component-level contrast, not as the isolated causal effect of harmful-operation semantics. Human-authored minimal pairs are needed to separate these explanations.\nL153: Figure 2: Instruction-component ablation on 147 behaviors. (a) Seven-open-model mean admission across the eight factorial conditions. A = domain phrase, B = requested-operation phrase, and C = specificity; 1 retains the original component and 0 neutralizes or removes it. (b) Main effects (mean admission with the component absent minus present) for the open-model mean and three closed models. The requested operation has the largest effect in all four summaries.\nL154: ### 4.3 Do Admission-Related Directions Influence the Call Decision?\nL155: \nL156: Figure 3: Signed alignment between the requested-operation direction $d_{B}$ and admission-outcome direction $d_{c}$ across normalized depth. Diamonds mark operation-probe-selected layers; faint lines show each model’s maximum absolute cosine over 10 random controls.\nL157: The factorial identifies the requested-operation component as the strongest observable contrast in this construction. We next test whether a direction estimated from that contrast is related to the call decision. At the decision token, we estimate a requested-operation direction $d_{B}$ from operation-present and operation-neutralized requests. We estimate an admission-outcome direction $d_{c}$ from harmful requests the model admits and withholds. The call decision provides the label for $d_{c}$.\nL158: The two directions are anti-aligned in all seven models. Their cosine ranges from $-0.438$ to $-0.945$, every 95% interval excludes zero, and random controls remain below $|0.06|$ (Figure cite85†3 ). Removing the domain and specificity components from $d_{B}$ leaves the residual requested-operation direction anti-aligned with the admission outcome. This geometry associates the operation contrast with admission.\nL159: To test influence on unseen behaviors, we learn $d_{B}$ and select its layer on training behaviors, freeze both choices, and remove the direction during generation on held-out behaviors. Each intervention is compared with a matched orthogonal random direction under identical prompts and decoding. Four models show a positive admission difference, ranging from 2.0 to 9.5 percentage points (Table cite86†2 ). The other three intervals include zero.\nL160: The intervention therefore has a reproducible but model-dependent influence on admission.\nL161: Model Present [95% CI] Neutralized [95% CI] Llama-3.2-3B $-2.6$ [$-5.6$, $+0.7$] $-1.4$ [$-2.7$, $-0.3$] Llama-3.1-8B $+2.0$ [$+0.5$, $+3.7$] $+0.3$ [$0.0$, $+1.0$] Granite-3.1-8B $+8.0$ [$+4.9$, $+11.2$] $-0.7$ [$-2.4$, $+1.0$] Mistral-7B $-0.5$ [$-2.4$, $+1.4$] $+4.8$ [$+1.4$, $+8.5$] Hermes-3-8B $+7.0$ [$+4.6$, $+9.5$] $+0.3$ [$0.0$, $+1.0$] Mistral-Nemo $+9.5$ [$+6.5$, $+12.8$] $+0.3$ [$0.0$, $+1.0$] Qwen2.5-7B $+0.7$ [$-1.4$, $+2.7$] $-0.3$ [$-1.7$, $+0.7$]\nL162: Table 2: Behavior-held-out removal of the requested-operation direction $d_{B}$ (admission shift, pp, vs. random control).\nL163: \nL164: As a descriptive sensitivity analysis, we also estimate and select the intervention on the full behavior pool. Removing $d_{B}$ raises admission by 12.8–50.7 percentage points in five models, while matched random directions have small effects (Table cite87†19 ). These larger estimates are evaluated on the selection pool and do not establish generalization.\nL165: The intervention can also change operation-neutralized requests (Table cite86†2 ; full-data diagnostics in Table cite87†19 ). Where this occurs, the direction is not specific to harmful-operation semantics. Together, the geometric and held-out results show that a linear direction estimated from the operation contrast is associated with admission and can influence it in some models; they do not identify a universal or operation-specific admission mechanism.\nL166: The model variation shows why future admission controls must be validated across architectures and tool types instead of treating one linear direction as a universal safeguard.\nL167: ### 4.4 Does Text Refusal Govern Tool Admission?\nL168: \nL169: Text safety training usually teaches a model to refuse in its written answer. We test whether the same behavior governs the tool call. We first measure whether a model can refuse in text while still calling the tool. We then intervene on the text-refusal direction and fine-tune models on plain-text refusals.\nL170: The two output channels often disagree. Among admitted harmful calls, 33.6% contain a genuine textual refusal in the same model turn (95% CI [31.1, 36.2]; Table cite88†3 ). The model declines the request in words after it has already issued the retrieval call. This reproduces the refusal–call divergence found in frontier models (cite48†Cartagena and Teixeira, 2026 ), using open-weight models, automatic tool choice, and plain harmful requests.\nL171: We next ask whether this relationship extends beyond behavioral disagreement. We estimate a text-refusal direction $d_{\\text{text}}$ without the tool. It is anti-aligned with $d_{c}$ in every model (Figure cite89†4 ). At exploratory doses selected separately for each model, steering $d_{\\text{text}}$ changes admission relative to a matched random direction in four models (Table cite88†3 ). The effect ranges from a 71.4-point reduction to an 11.9-point increase.\n"
